<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.7.2: Explaining Acceptable Moves to Solve an Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00169</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.7.2: Explaining Acceptable Moves to Solve an Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fbe66b1c-8d96-4528-b3fa-5d1786b49482</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>Here are some pairs of equations. While one partner listens, the other partner should:</para>
      <list>
        <item>Choose a pair of equations from column A. Explain why, if \( x \) is a number that
          makes the first equation true, then it also makes the second equation true.</item>
        <item>Choose a pair of equations from column B. Explain why the second equation is no longer
          true for a value of \( x \) that makes the first equation true.</item>
      </list>
      <para>Then, switch roles until you run out of time or you run out of pairs of equations.</para>
      <para>Some principles you might use to complete this activity include the following:</para>
      <list>
        <item>Distributive Property</item>
        <item>Commutative Property</item>
        <item>Addition Property of Equality</item>
        <item>Additive Inverse</item>
        <item>Subtraction Property of Equality</item>
        <item>Multiplication Property of Equality</item>
        <item>Division Property of Equality</item>
      </list>

        <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable unnumbered">
          <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>1.</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>\( 16=4(9-x) \)\( 16=36-4x \)</entry>
              <entry>\( 9x = 5x + 4 \)\( 14x = 4 \)</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>2.</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>\( 5x = 24 + 2x \)\( 3x = 24 \)</entry>
              <entry>\( \frac12x - 8 = 9 \)\( x - 8 = 18 \)</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>3.</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>\( -3(2x +9)=12 \)\( 2x+9=-4 \)</entry>
              <entry>\( 6x - 6 = 3x \)\( x - 1 = 3x \)</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>4.</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>\( 5x=3-x \)\( 5x = -x + 3 \)</entry>
              <entry>\( -11(x -2) = 8 \)\( x-2 = 8 + 11 \)</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>5.</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>\( 18 = 3x - 6 + x \)\( 18 = 4x - 6 \)</entry>
              <entry>\( 4 - 5x = 24 \)\( 5x = 20 \)</entry>
            </row>
          </tbody></tgroup></table>

      <para>After you have discussed these equations with your partner, answer questions 1 - 2.</para>


      <!--Susan's
      Work-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Which pair of equations did you select from Column A? Explain your
            reasoning.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>

          <para>Column A: For example:</para>

          <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
            <item>By the distributive property, \( 4(9−x) \) is equal to \( 36−4x \) for any value
              of \( x\). If \( 4(9−x) \) is equal to \(16\), then \( 36−4x \) is also equal to
              \(16\).</item>
            <item>If the two sides of the equation are equal, then they are still equal if the same
              amount, in this case \( 2x \), is subtracted from each side.</item>
            <item>\( -3(2x+9) \) divided by \( -3\) is \( 2x+9 \) and \( 12 \) divided by -\( 3 \)
              is \( -4 \). Because \( -3(2x+9) \) is equal to \( 12 \), dividing each of them by \(
              -3 \) gives the same result, so it is true that \( 2x+9 \) is equal \( −4 \).</item>
            <item>Subtracting by a number is equivalent to adding its opposite, so \( 3−x \) is
              equivalent to \( 3+(-x) \). Changing the order in which we add two numbers doesn't
              change the sum, per commutative property of addition. If \( 5x \) is equal to \( 3+-x
              \), then it is also equal to \( -x+3 \).</item>
            <item>Using the commutative property, \( 3x−6+x \) can be rewritten as \( 3x+x−6 \).
              Combining like terms gives an equivalent expression, \( 4x−6 \), so \( 4x−6 \) is also
              equal to \( 18 \).</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Which pair of equations did you select from Column B? Explain your
            reasoning.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>

          <para>Column B: For example:</para>

          <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
            <item>If the expressions \( 9x \) and \( 5x+4 \) are equal, then adding \( 5x \) to one
              expression and subtracting \( 5x \) from the other make the two expressions unequal.</item>
            <item>The 9 on the right side of the equation is multiplied by 2. If the expression on
              the left side was also multiplied by 2, the two sides would stay equal, but only a
              part of the expression on the left is doubled, so the two sides of the equation are no
              longer equal.</item>
            <item>If two expressions are equal, dividing one side by 6 but not dividing the other
              side by 6 makes them unequal.</item>
            <item>The left side of the first equation is divided by \( -11 \) but the right side is
              being added by 11. Performing two different operations with different numbers on the
              same value may not keep an equation true.</item>
            <item>Subtracting 4 from two values that are equal keeps the equation true. The
              expression on the left was multiplied (or divided) by \( -1 \), however, the
              expression on the right was not, so the two expressions are no longer equal.</item>
          </list>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>

    <section>
      <note class="self-check">
      <para>Select all of the following equations that are equivalent to \( 3(x - 2) = 18 \).</para>
      </note>
    </section>


    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section>
        <title>Solving Equations and Creating Equivalent Equations</title>
        <para>One way to create equivalent equations is to solve the equation. In each step of
          solving, an equivalent equation is created.</para>
        <para>Here is a general strategy for solving linear equations:</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
        <para>Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like terms.</para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
          equation.</para>
        <para>Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the
          equation.</para>
        <para>Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
        <para>Use the Multiplication or Division Property of Equality. State the solution to the
          equation.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Check the solution.</para>
        <para>Substitute the solution into the original equation to make sure the result is a true
          statement.</para>
  
          <section>
        <title>Example 1</title>
        <para>Here is an example of solving equations. Notice that each step is an acceptable move
          that creates an equivalent equation.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
        <para>Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-6(x+3)&amp;=&amp;24 \\ -6x-18&amp;=&amp;24 \end{array}\)</para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
          equation.</para>
        <para>Nothing to do. All the variables of \(x\) are on the left side.</para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the
          equation.</para>
        <para>Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-6x-18 {\style{color:red}+}
          {\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}8}&amp;=&amp;24 {\style{color:red}+}
          {\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}8} \\ -6x &amp;=&amp;42\end{array}\)</para>

        <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
        <para>Use the Multiplication or Division Property of Equality.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{-6x}{6}&amp;=&amp;\frac{42}{-6} \\ x&amp;=&amp;7 \end{array}\)</para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Check the solution.</para>
        <para>Substitute the solution into the original equation to make sure the result is a true
          statement.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-6(x+3)&amp;=&amp;24 \\
          -6({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}7}+3)&amp;=&amp;24 \\ -6(-4)&amp;=&amp;24
          \\24&amp;=&amp;24 \checkmark \end{array}\)</para>
  </section>
  <section>
        <title>Example 2</title>
        <para>Find the solution for \(3(4x - 1) - 2 = 8x + 3\).</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3(4x - 1) - 2&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 \\ 12x - 3 - 2&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 \\
          12x - 5&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 \\\end{array}\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
          equation.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}12x - 5 - 8x&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 - 8x \\ 4x - 5 &amp;=&amp;
          3\\\end{array}\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the
          equation.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}4x - 5 + 5 &amp;=&amp; 3 + 5 \\ 4x &amp;=&amp; 8\end{array}\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{4x}{4} &amp;=&amp; \frac84 \\ x &amp;=&amp; 2\end{array}\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3(4x - 1) - 2&amp;=&amp;8x + 3 ? \\ 3(4(2) - 1) - 2&amp;=&amp;8(2) +
          3 ? \\ 3(8 - 1) - 2&amp;=&amp;16 + 3 ? \\ 3(7) - 2&amp;=&amp;19 ? \\ 21 - 2&amp;=&amp;19 ?
          \\ 19&amp;=&amp;19 \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
  </section>
  <section>
        <para>Example 3</para>
        <para>Solve \(12x + 3(x + 7) = 10x - 24\).</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}12x + 3(x + 7) = 10x - 24 \\ 12x + 3x + 21 = 10x - 24 \\ 15x + 21 =
          10x - 24 \end{array}\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
          equation.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}15x + 21 -10x&amp;=&amp;10x-24-10x \\ 5x + 21&amp;=&amp;-
          24\end{array}\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the
          equation.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}5x+21-21&amp;=&amp;-24-21 \\ 5x&amp;=&amp;-45\end{array}\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{5x}{5}&amp;=&amp;-\frac{45}{5} \\ x &amp;=&amp;
          -9\end{array}\)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}12x + 3(x + 7)&amp;=&amp;10x - 24 ? \\ 12(-9) + 3(-9 +
          7)&amp;=&amp;10(-9) - 24 ? \\ -108 + 3(-2)&amp;=&amp;-90 - 24 ? \\ -108 + -6&amp;=&amp;-86 ?
          \\ -114&amp;=&amp;-114 \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
  </section>
</section>

<section>
        <title>Video: Acceptable Moves</title>
        <para>Watch the following video to learn more about acceptable moves.</para>
        <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
          <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/84fd718f1e7fa4c23f38cb1f2a45b7a21744d55f"/>
        </media>

        <note class="try">
      <title>Try It: Solving Equations and Creating Equivalent Equations</title>

          <exercise>
            <problem>
              <para>Write two equivalent equations to \( 2(x+4)= 12 \).</para>
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
                compare your work.</para>
  
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
              <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
  
              <list>
                <item>\(2x + 8 = 24 \)</item>
                <item>\(x + 4 = 6 \)</item>
                <item>\(2x = 16\)</item>
              </list>
  
            </solution>
          </exercise>

          <exercise>
            <problem>
              <para>Solve \(2(x+4)=12\).</para>
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
                compare your work.</para>
  
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
              <para>
                <emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.
                Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses.</para>
  
              <para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}2(x + 4) &amp;=&amp; 12 \\ 2x + 8 &amp;= &amp;12\end{array}\)</para>
  
              <para>
                <emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
                equation. Nothing to do. All the variables of x are on the left side.</para>
  
              <para>
                <emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the
                equation.</para>
  
              <para>Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
  
              <para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}2x + 8{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}8} &amp;=&amp;
                12{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}8 }\\ 2x &amp;=&amp; 4\end{array}\)</para>
  
              <para>
                <emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.
                Use the Multiplication or Division Property of Equality.</para>
  
              <para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{2x}{2} &amp;=&amp; \frac42 \\ x &amp;=&amp;
                2\end{array}\)</para>
  
              <para>
                <emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
  
              <para>Substitute the solution into the original equation to make sure the result is a
                true statement.</para>
  
              <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2(x + 4) &amp;=&amp; 12 \\ 2({\style{color:red}2} + 4)
                &amp;=&amp; 12 \\ 2(6) &amp;=&amp; 12 \\ 12 &amp;=&amp; 12 \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>

          <exercise>
            <problem>
              <para> Solve \(-12+8(x−5)=-4+3(5x-2) \)</para>
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
                compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as much as
                possible.</para>
  
              <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-12 + 8(x - 5) &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(5x - 2) \\ -12 + 8x - 40
                &amp;=&amp; -4 + 15x - 6 \\ 8x - 52 &amp;=&amp; 15x - 10\end{array}\)</para>
  
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the
                equation.</para>
  
              <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}8x - 52 - 15x &amp;=&amp; 15x - 10 - 15x \\ -7x -52
                &amp;=&amp; - 10\end{array}\)</para>
  
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of
                the equation.</para>
  
              <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-7x - 52 + 52 &amp;=&amp; -10 + 52 \\ -7x &amp;=&amp;
                42\end{array}\)</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to
                1.</para>
  
              <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{-7x}{-7} &amp;=&amp; \frac {42}{-7} \\ x &amp;=&amp;
                -6\end{array}\)</para>
  
              <para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
  
              <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-12 + 8(x - 5) &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(5x - 2) ? \\ -12 + 8(-6 - 5)
                &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(5(-6) - 2) ? \\ -12 + 8(-11) &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(-30 - 2) ? \\ -12 +
                -88 &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(-32) ? \\ -100 &amp;=&amp; -4 + -96 \\ -100 &amp;=&amp; -100 
                \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
  
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
        </section>
    </section>
